---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ExtLink from '../components/ExtLink.astro';
import { published } from '../data/util';
const pubs = (await published('publications')).sort((a, b) => b.data.year - a.data.year);
---
<Base title="Science" current="/science/" description="NeuroStat's research approach and areas: sleep and circadian health, memory and cognition, and neurodiagnostics.">
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Science</div>
      <h1>Science</h1>
      <p>We start with questions neuroscience has not yet answered.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Our approach</h2>
      <p>Most of our programmes started from a question nobody had answered well. Why does a memory that is still stored become impossible to reach? Why does thinking fall apart in a predictable order when sleep is cut short? Why do some people adapt to night shifts and others never do? We pick questions where a better answer would change treatment, then test the answer on the people who live with the problem.</p>
      <p>That often takes us to demanding places: offshore installations, polar stations, desert sites and military bases, and also hospital wards and sleep laboratories. People there are exposed to sleep loss, isolation and a disrupted body clock in ways that are easy to repeat and to measure. Access and logistics at these sites are handled by our group companies <ExtLink href="http://hiemalpolar.com">Hiemal Polar Services</ExtLink>, <ExtLink href="http://eremosdesert.com">Eremos Desert Operations</ExtLink> and <ExtLink href="http://aegiscorp.net">Aegis Corporation</ExtLink>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band alt" id="sleep">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research area</p>
      <h2>Sleep &amp; Circadian Health</h2>
      <p>Sleep is governed by two systems. One is a pressure that builds the longer we stay awake. The other is a clock in the suprachiasmatic nucleus that decides when we are ready to sleep. Shift work, travel and long polar rotations pull the two apart.</p>
      <p>Vesperil acts on the melatonin receptors MT1 and MT2 and helps with sleep timing. Lunavel blocks the orexin receptors and so lowers the drive to stay awake. At the moment we are working on resetting the clock after sudden shifts (NS-455) and on the grogginess that follows waking from deep sleep (NS-318).</p>
      <p><a href="/products/">Products</a> · <a href="/pipeline/">Pipeline</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="memory">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research area</p>
      <h2>Memory &amp; Cognition</h2>
      <p>Forgetting is sometimes a storage problem and sometimes a retrieval problem. After some head injuries and after severe stress, the evidence points to memories that are still stored but can no longer be reached. In animals, such memories have been recovered by reactivating the neurons that hold them, the memory engram.</p>
      <p><b>NS-091 (retrevamide)</b> builds on that idea. It acts on a persistent molecular signature of silent engrams and reactivates only the group of neurons that holds the lost memory, leaving other memories alone. It is in a Phase III study in patients with retrograde amnesia after trauma.</p>
      <p>NS-760 (adravacetam) is aimed at a different problem: keeping attention and working memory steady through long isolation and broken sleep. It is being tested in <a href="/clinical-trials/boreas-2/">BOREAS-2</a>.</p>
      <h3>Selected background literature</h3>
      <ol class="small" style="padding-left:1.2rem">
        <li>Liu X, Ramirez S, Pang PT, et al. Optogenetic stimulation of a hippocampal engram activates fear memory recall. <i>Nature</i> 484, 381–385 (2012). <ExtLink href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nature11028">doi:10.1038/nature11028</ExtLink></li>
        <li>Ryan TJ, Roy DS, Pignatelli M, Arons A, Tonegawa S. Engram cells retain memory under retrograde amnesia. <i>Science</i> 348, 1007–1013 (2015). <ExtLink href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaa5542">doi:10.1126/science.aaa5542</ExtLink></li>
        <li>Roy DS, Arons A, Mitchell TI, et al. Memory retrieval by activating engram cells in mouse models of early Alzheimer's disease. <i>Nature</i> 531, 508–512 (2016). <ExtLink href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nature17172">doi:10.1038/nature17172</ExtLink></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band alt" id="neurodiagnostics">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research area</p>
      <h2>Neurodiagnostics</h2>
      <p>Measuring sleep and cognition usually means a trip to a laboratory. Halo and Baseline bring the measurement to the workplace. Halo records EEG with dry electrodes. Baseline compares attention, memory and decision making with the same person's earlier results. Occupational physicians use both, and the data feed back into our research. In the laboratory we use larger instruments: Fathom, a head scanner with a 7 tesla magnet, Diadem, a helmet that records brain activity while a person moves, and Tenor, which stimulates a small region of the brain with ultrasound.</p>
      <p><a href="/products/halo/">NeuroStat Halo</a> · <a href="/products/baseline/">NeuroStat Baseline</a> · <a href="/products/fathom/">NeuroStat Fathom</a> · <a href="/products/diadem/">NeuroStat Diadem</a> · <a href="/products/tenor/">NeuroStat Tenor</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Publications and posters</h2>
      {pubs.length === 0
        ? <p class="muted">No publications are listed at the moment.</p>
        : (
          <ul class="list-plain">
            {pubs.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <span class="tag">{p.data.kind}</span>
                <p style="margin:.5rem 0 0"><b>{p.data.title}</b><br /><span class="muted small">{p.data.authors}. {p.data.venue}, {p.data.year}.</span></p>
                {p.data.file && <p class="small"><a href={p.data.file}>Download PDF</a></p>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )}
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
